r  people in the labour party  aren't anti-semitic themselves they are  enabling it or tolerating it within  their ranks  well all i can say is like some of my  colleagues around the shadow cabinet so  john mcdonald for example  you know we have dealt with this problem  too slowly and inadequately  it is obvious that even the changes  we've made  have have not worked we still have  people  using anti-semitic language and  uh being very very unpleasant uh  in this area and i'm not going to take  it uh  i felt personal shame and i thought it  was the most shameful day in the history  of my party  when luciana berger left us she is a  young  pregnant woman bullied out by racist  thugs  and that is completely unacceptable and  you know  when she did that at that point i  realized that i had to step up and i'm  going to be calling this out  day in day out as long as it continues  why not suspend her  constituency labour talking well i've  asked the  general secretary of the party to  investigate goings-on and actually i've  asked the general secretary of the party  to investigate allegations of bullying  in the neighboring constituency  liverpool riverside  where my colleague louise elman  has been the subject of anti-semitic  abuse as well  i mean the people who left were all from  the labour party were all people who  wanted  a second referendum as well so  now that the lab party shifted his  position do you think that was a sort of  a vain gesture if you like or was it  actually what forced the party to shift  its position  for fear of others going the same way i  know there's a lot of complexity and  confusion around it i didn't want anyone  to leave let me just  say that uh but they didn't force us  into this position in fact  our position was it was very sort of  clear  but you and i know that mr corbin and  his office were pretty reluctant about  it  there's definitely levels of sort of  enthusiasm for the second referendum  amongst my colleagues but the conference  decision  negotiated by kia with our members  was very clear there's a number of  hurdles we needed to approach we  preferred a meaningful vote  we would prefer this to be debated in a  general election  we failed on trying to get to there when  theresa may  offered potential concessions we talked  to her we  told her what our red lines were we  prepared to help her with a deal if she  could get closer to  economic alignment we tried to do that  in the chamber last night and we lost  that vote  and so now the final part of our policy  has been triggered which is for this to  be resolved  uh in a people's vote so our policy has  been consistent  by the way that isn't going to stop us  also trying to see whether we can reach  a concessionary position with the  government even though it was rejected  by  parliament last night and how worried  are you about  further splits in the labour party i'm  very worried about it  i i think there are still a number of my  colleagues who are perilously close to  leaving  and i've described this as a battle for  the soul of the labour party  and for lots of reasons all of them  understandable  our front bench you know  is a sort of made up of people who were  prepared to serve under jeremy  when there was a leadership challenge  two years ago and i've been arguing for  some time now that we need to sort of  change the balance on the front bench so  that those  the different traditions that have  formed the labour party can be  more representative do you think they  come back the yvette coopers and people  i i honestly don't know but i do think  we need to sort of go through a process  of trying to  get people back on the front bed if it  can't happen  uh for whatever reason whether jeremy  feels very comfortable with the people  he's got on the front bench now or those  people don't want to come back  that's fine and so what i've suggested  you know is a different way of doing it  which is to bring my colleagues together  who represent  the social democratic tradition to allow  them their own sort of policy space that  they can feed into the policy  let's take something like the suspension  of chris williamson i mean mr corbin was  against that wasn't he  i don't uh actually i don't know what  jeremy's position is  the general secretary is the one that uh  suspended uh  chris williams said i wrote to her and  our chief whip  yesterday morning to say that i  thought there was a strong case that  chris williamson had bought the party  astonishing if you think that and you  don't even know if the labour leader  agrees with you  well i've not spoken to him because he  was in the chamber dealing with the  brexit debate  i have asked him to come and see me next  week where we can talk about what  further measures we can take to  eradicate anti-semitism in the party and  that's the big thing for me  i mean i remember the day you became  deputy leader just uh  over parliament square there you made it  clear that you saw yourself as the  keeper of the conscience of  of all broad labor if i can call it that  rather rather than carbonite labor  could there ever come a point where you  say i simply can't remain in this party  i joined the labour party when i was 15.  you know 200 000 people have made me  deputy leader you would have to drive me  out of the labour party yeah  could you be driven do you think yeah  well they could give it a good go but  that is a good you know i'll be fighting  back if they do  you're not you're not threatening all  the time to leave if you don't get i've  never threatened to leave the labour  party no i mean  it's deputy leader i want i want a  viable electable labor government  and i spent three years as deputy leader  to try and bring everyone together  and you know the tragedy for me the  honest truth is those mps that left  i think we could have avoided that  splinter  and we could certainly have done more to  support my colleague lucy anna berger  before she made t